{"name":"January Dawn","hex":"#dfe2e5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dawn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To begin to brighten with daylight.","To start to appear or become obvious."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The morning twilight period immediately before sunrise.","The rising of the sun."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dawn"}],"entries":[{"title":"January","blocks":[{"text":"January is the first month of the year in the Julian and Gregorian calendars. Its length is 31 days. The first day of the month is known as New Year's Day. It is, on average, the coldest month of the year within most of the Northern Hemisphere and the warmest month of the year within most of the Southern Hemisphere. In the Southern hemisphere, January is the seasonal equivalent of July in the Northern hemisphere and vice versa."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January","wikidata":"Q108"},{"title":"Dawn","blocks":[{"text":"Dawn is the time that marks the beginning of twilight before sunrise. It is recognized by the appearance of indirect daylight being scattered in Earth's atmosphere, when the centre of the Sun's disc has reached 18° below the observer's horizon. This morning twilight period will last until sunrise, when direct daylight outshines the diffused light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Desert_Dawn.jpg/330px-Desert_Dawn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dawn","wikidata":"Q11326182"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Lusenberg-wise-men.jpg/500px-Lusenberg-wise-men.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Lusenberg-wise-men.jpg/1280px-Lusenberg-wise-men.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/